    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122">

                <pb facs="0148" n="141" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg141"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8716: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-12T15:00:00+08:00">July 12, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 12—10:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>265. My 263, July 12, 10 a.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>An Australian press correspondent today telephoned Atcheson<anchor n="65"
                            xml:id="d122-f1"/> asking his opinion regarding the results which would
                        follow if the Chinese Government were to suggest to the British and American
                            <pb facs="0149" n="142" xml:id="pg142"/> Governments the advisability of
                        representations from them to the Japanese and Chinese Governments urging
                        moderation in the present crisis. He observed that a favorable opportunity
                        for such representations in the case of the British was presented by the
                        present Anglo-Japanese conversations in London and that similar steps taken
                        by the American Government in the past afforded a precedent.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The correspondent named was Timperley, representing <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Manchester Guardian</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Christian Science Monitor</hi>. Significance is given his inquiry by
                        his known collaboration in drafting of the Foreign Office statement
                        yesterday (see my 261, July 12, 8 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >p.m.</hi>]<anchor n="65a" xml:id="d122-f2"/> and his relations with Hu
                        Shih and other leading intellectuals influential with the Foreign
                        Office.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Atcheson replied that he did not know what the reaction in Washington
                        would be to such a suggestion and that he assumed the Chinese Ambassador in
                        Washington knew by now how the ground lay.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>To the Department and Peiping.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
